wifi: mt76: mt7615: end rate sets sent at 160 MHz with a robust rate

A frame gets two tries in each slot of its rate set and 12 in all, so
one that starts in slot 0 ends in slot 5, with the third rate: minstrel's
most probable one. A set with a 160 MHz rate is sent at the bandwidth of
its probe or first rate, and its other rates are widened to it. That
turns the most probable rate, often a 20 or 40 MHz rate at range, into
one that needs up to 9 dB more signal (3 dB per doubling of the
bandwidth, IEEE 802.11ac-2013 22.3.19.1; MCS 6 needs -65 dBm at 20 MHz
and -56 dBm at 160 MHz). At range none of the 12 tries got through: in
tests between two MT7615 at -64 to -69 dBm, 21 of 29 (AP) and 47 of 50
(STA) frames sent with a set at 160 MHz were lost, and none of 376 sent
with a narrower set.

End a set sent at 160 MHz with the most robust rate there, one stream
MCS 0 (-73 dBm): as its last rate, which a frame reaches when the
hardware starts it at a later slot (it keeps its place in the set
between frames), and as its third rate too if minstrel's most probable
rate is narrower, as how that rate does at 160 MHz is unknown. In the
same tests nothing was lost afterwards: 0 of 368 frames, 77 of them
sent with a set at 160 MHz.

--- a/mt7615/mac.c
+++ b/mt7615/mac.c
@@ -1107,6 +1107,27 @@ mt7615_mac_update_rate_desc(struct mt761
 		if (rates[i].flags & IEEE80211_TX_RC_160_MHZ_WIDTH)
 			same_bw = true;
 
+	/*
+	 * With two tries per slot and 12 in all, a frame ends with the third
+	 * rate, minstrel's most probable one, or with the last one if the
+	 * hardware starts it at a later slot. Widened to 160 MHz, the most
+	 * probable rate can need up to 9 dB more signal than where it was
+	 * measured, so end a set sent at 160 MHz with the most robust rate
+	 * there, one stream MCS 0: as the last rate, and as the third one
+	 * too if that one is narrower.
+	 */
+	if (bw_flags & IEEE80211_TX_RC_160_MHZ_WIDTH) {
+		struct ieee80211_tx_rate tail = {
+			.count = rates[3].count,
+			.flags = IEEE80211_TX_RC_VHT_MCS | bw_flags,
+		};
+
+		ieee80211_rate_set_vht(&tail, 0, 1);
+		if (!(rates[2].flags & IEEE80211_TX_RC_160_MHZ_WIDTH))
+			rates[2] = tail;
+		rates[3] = tail;
+	}
+
 	if (same_bw && probe_rate)
 		mt7615_mac_rate_set_bw(ref, bw_flags);
 
